<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Picking a time for a group usually means a long thread and one person's best
    guess. In Ravocal, members mark when they're free, the group votes on the
    times they share, and the winner is the meeting. When there isn't enough
    overlap, the owner can force a vote that puts their own free times first.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Find the shared time</h3>
        <p>Each member marks the blocks they're free, and Ravocal finds the slots enough of the group has in common.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Vote with ranked choice</h3>
        <p>Members rank the overlapping slots instead of picking one, and the winning slot becomes the meeting.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Sign in without passwords</h3>
        <p>An emailed sign-in link, Google, Facebook, or your own OpenID Connect provider.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="start">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>mkdir ravocal &amp;&amp; cd ravocal
curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cubicecho/ravocal/main/docker-compose.yml
printf 'BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=%s\nSECURE_LOCAL_NET=true\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" &gt; .env
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then open <a href="http://localhost:3001">http://localhost:3001</a>. It pulls
      <a href="{{ site.docker }}" rel="noopener"><code>ghcr.io/cubicecho/ravocal</code></a>
      and Postgres, and migrations run at boot. <code>SECURE_LOCAL_NET</code> signs in
      by address alone, which is for your own machine; the README covers email
      links and the other sign-in methods.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
    <a href="{{ site.repo }}#readme" rel="noopener">{{ site.repo }}</a>
  </p>
</section>
